ould private  businesses define a threshold for when  to close a workplace  oh this is this is a spicy one um you  know i first  with many of the groups that i worked  with at the beginning  the threshold was just defined for them  by  when you run out of humans to do that  job you have to close  but then depending on the organization  um  groups started just figuring out or  having  quite a depth of people who could  replace those roles and so we don't want  to end up in a place where we are  creating a workplace environment where  spreading is actively happening all the  time you know and it's just a continuous  outbreak but we're just putting more and  more bodies in to replace the ones that  are in isolation we don't want that  um so i think that a threshold really is  um related to containment so that you  know it's okay to backfill positive  cases but if you're starting to see  tertiary and quaternary infections um  from a spreading event that happened in  the workplace then to me that flag's you  know an area for serious concern and  um  the  you know further investigation so this  is where many of the groups that i work  with and and i'm seeing this used by a  lot more epidemiologists or are what we  call precision epidemiology  investigations and that's where we use  genomic sequencing  to  sequence all of the positive cases  within a workplace that  are clustered around the same  period of time or maybe the same area of  a workplace or a production you know or  a team  and through sequencing we then just  compare the viruses to understand  how similar they are to each other  and estimate the likelihood that we  think that the transmission had happened  in the  the production or the team within the  workplace  or under or or be able to tell you know  oh these viruses are totally different  from each other you know we're pretty  sure that these two people just  separately became infected in their own  communities and then came into work  positive around the same point in time  and if it's the latter then great news  you know safety policies are working you  know people will become positive you  know from  home and community  but  then if you find out that oh these are  almost identical viruses  um  among this whole group of people  then that's the time where it becomes  the employer's responsibility to closely  review the safety protocols and make  revisions to patch them up and ensure  that a spreading event like that won't  happen again  or reduce the risk  thank you so much dr adamson we have  time for two more questions our next  question what do people need to do to  get back to work after natural infection  and the second part of this is when is a  10-day isolation negative test and  healthcare provider clearance necessary  so i mentioned that i i  you know really don't think that the  clearance is necessary for people who  were asymptomatic or who had mild  symptoms that that improved over time  but i definitely don't think we should  be gaining on having to have a negative  test before we go back to work i mean  i've seen people who are testing  positive for months and months sometimes  more than 100 days and we can't make  them be out of work for 100 days so  but i am seeing many union protocols  just out of you know not under  conceptually understanding the  difference between a positive pcr test  result and a level of virus that  of  you know active infectious virus that  could be a risk to others  yet in the same way  i do see many opportunities to bring  essential  employees back sometimes sooner than 10  days  now the  i think that this really is then gated  on multiple negative pcr tests 24 hours  apart at least  you know consecutively  so that if an individual does become  positive for example someone who's fully  vaccinated they might test positive for  one or two days and then go negative  they don't experience any symptoms at  all and the negative multiple negative  test results tell us that you know  they're not infectious to others  i don't consider that to be a false  positive case i really think it is  becoming more of a profile of what i see  among vaccinated breakthrough infections  and the 10-day isolation then is not  always absolutely necessary  thank you so much we have time for one  more question let's talk about children  should unvaccinated kids test to stay in  school instead of quarantine after  exposure and can parents of infected  children return to work  i love this dilemma um because  i think that with kids  the same concepts apply in school we  want great ventilation we want doors to  be open windows to the outside to be  open we want to bring air filters into  the classroom we want certain routine  surveillance testing to pick up kids as  soon as they're positive  but and we want masking you know i think  that masks are  some it's been amazing how tolerant  children have been  to to wearing masks when they go to  school  but you know we know the kids  are  still less likely to become infected  less likely to pass it on to other  people and experience symptoms and i  really do think that test to stay  is going to create more benefit for  children than  you know assuming that all children  should quarantine after an exposure in  the classroom i think that we have to  make we have to weigh the risks and the  benefits here for kids and i think that  you know the social emotional academic  development of kids is incredibly  important and we've seen this modeled  out in the uk not modeled out but  actually tested out in ran in a  randomized experiment  to show that  um  they they randomized um  schools so that some of them quarantined  all children when there was a positive  and some of them tested all of them  after an exposure and they found out  that that with testing to stay children  spend a meaningful  amount of additional days in the  classroom and they did not see  a higher rate of new cases because of it  and so if we can get the access  to affordable testing for school  districts i think this can be an  incredible tool to keep kids in the  classroom  absolutely thank you dr adamson for this  great presentation today and for  important research do you want to  provide any closing remarks before we go  no just thank you to lab roots for  inviting me to to be part of this uh  coronavirus event  and thank you again for your  presentation today and before we go i  want to thank our audience for joining  us today and for their interesting  questions questions we did not have time  for today and those submitted during the  on-demand period will be addressed via  the speaker  using your contact information you  provided at the time of registration and  this webcast can be viewed on demand for  two years until october 2023  labrits will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event until next time take care  everyone stay safe  bye
 

